/-!
# The rational Hecke-to-Dirichlet L-function dictionary

Over `ℚ`, every nonzero integral ideal of `𝓞 ℚ` is generated by its absolute norm, and every
modulus `𝔪 : Modulus ℚ` divides `(N)·∞` with the same finite prime support, where
`N = Ideal.absNorm 𝔪.finitePart`. Attaching the complex Dirichlet character
`η.toDirichletCharacter : DirichletCharacter ℂ N` to a ray class character `η : RayClassCharacter 𝔪`
identifies the two theories at every level — ideal weights, norm-regrouped coefficients, deleted
Euler factors, primitive characters, continued `L`-functions, meromorphic divisors, and zero counts:

* For every nonzero ideal `I` of `𝓞 ℚ`, `η.toMultiplicativeIdealWeight I` equals
  `η.toDirichletCharacter (Ideal.absNorm I)` whether or not `I` is prime to `𝔪`: at ideals meeting
  `𝔪.support` both sides vanish. Regrouping by norm therefore gives
  `normCoeff ℚ η.toUnitaryIdealWeight.toIdealArithmeticFunction =
    toArithmeticFunction (η.toDirichletCharacter ·)` as arithmetic functions on all of `ℕ`.
* Mapping height-one primes of `𝓞 ℚ` to their absolute norms identifies `𝔪.support` with
  `N.primeFactors`, so `η.eulerCorrection 𝔫 s` equals both
  `∏ p ∈ (Ideal.absNorm 𝔫.finitePart).primeFactors \ N.primeFactors,
    (1 - η.toDirichletCharacter p * p ^ (-s))` and the full product over
  `(Ideal.absNorm 𝔫.finitePart).primeFactors`.
* Bundled primitive ray class characters `PrimitiveRayClassCharacter ℚ` correspond bijectively to
  primitive complex Dirichlet characters `(N : ℕ+) × {χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N // χ.IsPrimitive}`
  (`PrimitiveRayClassCharacter.ratEquiv`), sending even primitive characters to finite moduli `(N)`
  without the real place and odd primitive characters to `(N)·∞`.
* On `ℂ`, `heckeLFunctionC η` is the meromorphic normal form of `η.toDirichletCharacter.LFunction`.
  Its punctured germ at every `s : ℂ` is that of `η.toDirichletCharacter.LFunction`; for `η ≠ 1` the
  two functions are equal on the whole plane, while for `η = 1` they agree at every `s ≠ 1` (at the
  pole `s = 1`, `heckeLFunctionC` takes its normal-form value `0` whereas `LFunction` is an
  unnormalized Hurwitz combination). Punctured-germ agreement transports `meromorphicOrderAt`,
  `MeromorphicOn.divisor`, `divisorCount`, and `zeroCount` on arbitrary sets without excluding `1`,
  and connects critical-strip zero counts of `heckeLFunctionC` to `dirichletData.entireCompletion`
  and `zetaEntireCompletion`.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.LFunctions.PrimitiveRayClassCharacter.toDirichletCharacter`: the primitive Dirichlet
  character attached to a bundled primitive ray class character of `ℚ`.
* `TauCeti.LFunctions.PrimitiveRayClassCharacter.ofDirichletCharacter`: the bundled primitive ray
  class character of `ℚ` underlying any complex Dirichlet character.
* `TauCeti.LFunctions.PrimitiveRayClassCharacter.ratEquiv`: equivalence between
  `PrimitiveRayClassCharacter ℚ` and `(N : ℕ+) × {χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ N // χ.IsPrimitive}`.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.GlobalNumberFields.RayClassCharacter.normCoeff_toUnitaryIdealWeight`: norm regrouping of
  a rational ray class character weight is `toArithmeticFunction (η.toDirichletCharacter ·)`.
* `TauCeti.GlobalNumberFields.RayClassCharacter.eulerCorrection_eq_prod_sdiff_primeFactors` and
  `TauCeti.GlobalNumberFields.RayClassCharacter.eulerCorrection_eq_prod_primeFactors`: rational
  deleted Euler factors in terms of prime factors of modulus norms.
* `TauCeti.LFunctions.heckeLFunctionC_eventuallyEq_LFunction`,
  `TauCeti.LFunctions.heckeLFunctionC_eq_LFunction`, and
  `TauCeti.LFunctions.heckeLFunctionC_eq_LFunction_of_ne_one`: comparison of `heckeLFunctionC` over
  `ℚ` with `DirichletCharacter.LFunction`.
* `TauCeti.LFunctions.divisor_heckeLFunctionC_eq_LFunction`,
  `TauCeti.LFunctions.zeroCount_heckeLFunctionC_eq_LFunction`, and
  `TauCeti.LFunctions.zeroCount_dirichletData_entireCompletion_eq_heckeLFunctionC_inter`: divisor
  and zero-count transport.

## References

* J. Neukirch, *Algebraic Number Theory*, Chapter VI, §1 and Chapter VII, §6.
* N. Childress, *Class Field Theory*, Chapter V, §1.
* H. Davenport, *Multiplicative Number Theory*, 3rd ed., Chapters 4–5 and 12.
-/

public section
noncomputable section

open ArithmeticFunction DirichletCharacter Filter IsDedekindDomain NumberField Set
